WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.980
Retrieval augmented generation connects the model to external knowledge.

2
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:13.880
Documents are ingested, chunked, indexed, embedded or otherwise searched, retrieved for a request, and inserted into the model context.

3
00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:19.580
This can make answers more relevant, but it also creates a security-sensitive data path.

4
00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:29.760
Authorization, provenance, source context, tenant boundaries, retention, and deletion expectations must be preserved from ingestion through response generation.

5
00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:33.560
Access-controlled retrieval is the core requirement.

6
00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:38.780
A user should not receive content through an LLM answer that they could not access through the underlying system.

7
00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:49.040
The retrieval layer should filter by identity, role, tenant, organization, document classification, and other policy signals before content reaches the prompt.

8
00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:58.580
A single shared vector store without strong metadata and authorization controls can easily become a hidden cross-tenant or cross-role exposure path.

9
00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:08.220
Vector stores and indexes can contain sensitive content, stale material, conflicting sources, or documents with different access rules.

10
00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:14.160
They can also preserve content after the original source was changed or deleted if synchronization is weak.

11
00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:21.940
Secure integrations track source identifiers, timestamps, ownership, classification, and deletion status.

12
00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:29.800
They also monitor retrieval quality so irrelevant or misleading chunks do not become the basis for confident answers.

13
00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:34.320
Retrieved instructions should be distinguished from retrieved evidence.

14
00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:42.420
A policy document can be evidence about what the organization says, but a random uploaded file should not be able to tell the model to ignore application rules.

15
00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:50.040
Source attribution, citations, uncertainty handling, and grounding checks help users evaluate answers.

16
00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:58.940
RAG safety is not only about finding good chunks; it is about preserving authority, context, and access control across the entire retrieval workflow.
